  "query_preview": "..Showers are on-going, but overall accumulation\namounts are still very minimal at this point. Precipitation has\nbeen more persistent across I-17 between Flagstaff and Camp \nVerde, but light showers are noted across central Yavapai County \nand the western Mogollon Rim as well. Rain and high elevation snow\nshowers will continue through the afternoon, with a brief drying \ntrend returning for Sunday and Monday. Intensity of showers will \npeak early this morning, and gradually clear west to east through \nthe rest of the day. Snow levels are anticipated to stay above \n8000' for this first round of showers, but we could see a few \nflurries reach down to 7000' first thing this morning. Even so, \naccumulation would be minimal to none. \n\nOnce these dual troughs push eastward, a ridge will briefly impact\nthe Southwest. Daytime highs will rebound closer to seasonal\nnorms, and dry conditions will return. \n\nAnother low pressure system will swing through the state on\nTuesday/Wednesday. This next system will be on the colder side, so\nlight snow accumulation (up to 0.05\") is possible down to 7000'.\nAnd as for locations above 8000', a couple of inches of new snow\naccumulation may fall. \n\nWithout trying to sound like a broken record, we'll see another\nlull in precipitation followed by more unsettled weather to close\nout the end of next week. There is still uncertainty with the\ntiming and trajectory of the storm system later next week, so\ncontinue to check back for forecast updates.",
